  {"id":122193,"slug":"gryllus-pennsylvanicus","sci":"Gryllus pennsylvanicus","common":"Fall Field Cricket","family":"Gryllidae","obs":687,"regions":[],"months":[7,8,9,10],"photos":[{"id":55363852,"ext":"jpg","by":"Declan McCabe","lic":"CC BY"},{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Gryllus_pennsylvanicus_male_01.jpg/1280px-Gryllus_pennsylvanicus_male_01.jpg","by":"Kevinjudge","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gryllus_pennsylvanicus_male_01.jpg"},{"id":103117080,"ext":"jpg","by":"Sarah MacGregor","lic":"CC BY"},{"id":97172481,"ext":"jpeg","by":"an iNaturalist observer","lic":"CC0"},{"id":227657576,"ext":"jpg","by":"Jenny Kirkpatrick","lic":"CC BY"},{"id":158942077,"ext":"jpeg","by":"Marvin Eng","lic":"CC BY-NC"}],"wiki":"Gryllus pennsylvanicus","summary":"Gryllus pennsylvanicus is known as the fall field cricket. G. pennsylvanicus is common in southern Ontario, is widespread across much of North America and can be found even into parts of northern Mexico. It tends to be absent in most of the southwestern United States including southern California. Within its geographic range this field cricket will burrow into soil in fields and forest edges."},
  {"id":51493,"slug":"trimerotropis-pallidipennis","sci":"Trimerotropis pallidipennis","common":"Pallid-winged Grasshopper","family":"Acrididae","obs":583,"regions":["north-cascades","central-cascades","rainier","gorge","olympics","oregon-cascades"],"months":[6,7,8,9,10],"photos":[{"id":2173526,"ext":"JPG","by":"James Bailey","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Trimerotropis.jpg/1280px-Trimerotropis.jpg","by":"Mike","lic":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trimerotropis.jpg"},{"id":156160111,"ext":"jpeg","by":"Gavin Slater","lic":"CC BY"},{"id":437493756,"ext":"jpeg","by":"leewarren","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":437493791,"ext":"jpeg","by":"leewarren","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":437481246,"ext":"jpeg","by":"leewarren","lic":"CC BY-NC"}],"wiki":"Trimerotropis pallidipennis","summary":"The pallid-winged grasshopper is a common grasshopper of the family Acrididae, native to the deserts of western North America from British Columbia to Argentina. They are more active during the summer months, and their pale, mottled coloration makes them hard to see against surfaces such as the granite often found in the gravel of dry river beds. They grow to be 37 millimetres."},
